From 9cd67c03f1019069515813038618c18c5d509d70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:47:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] clpi_access_point(): check if ep_fine table is empty before
 use

CVE-2026-112282. _parse_ep_map_stream() clamps a coarse entry's
ref_ep_fine_id to num_ep_fine - 1 only when num_ep_fine > 0. A ClipInfo
file whose EP map declares coarse entries and no fine entries keeps the
18-bit value read from the file (up to 262143) in every coarse entry,
and entry->fine is whatever malloc(0) returned. clpi_access_point()
then reads entry->fine[ref] in its first loop, and entry->fine[jj]
further down, past that allocation: a heap out-of-bounds read reached
on a seek or an angle change in a crafted BDMV/CLIPINF/*.clpi (disc,
image or folder).

clpi_lookup_spn() got the same check in "clpi_lookup_spn(): check if
ep_fine table is empty before use" (f69ecfc3f26c, part of 1.5.1).
clpi_access_point() did not, and libbluray's master (448f89609163,
2026-10-09) still lacks it.

Why this bound is the whole fix: every fine index the function uses is
a ref_ep_fine_id of a coarse entry, or lies in [start, end) where start
and end are such refs or num_ep_fine. When num_ep_fine >= 1 the parser
has already clamped each ref to num_ep_fine - 1, so those indexes are in
range; only num_ep_fine == 0 escapes the clamp.

What the callers do with the early return (time 0, packet 0, the same
values as the existing early return for a clip without an EP map):
- nav_clip_packet_search() (called by nav_packet_search() for bd_seek()
  and bd_tell_time(), and by _update_time_psr_from_stream() and
  _init_textst_timer()) clamps the packet to clip->start_pkt and keeps a
  zero time, so nav_packet_search() reports time 0: a seek lands at the
  start of the clip and the reported time is the clip's start.
- nav_clip_angle_change_search() (bd_seamless_angle_change()) stores 0
  as angle_change_pkt; the read loop (_bd_read()) then changes the angle
  at the current packet and calls _clip_seek_time(bd, 0).
Both are what libbluray already does for a clip whose CPI has no stream
entry.
---
 src/libbluray/bdnav/clpi_parse.c | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/libbluray/bdnav/clpi_parse.c b/src/libbluray/bdnav/clpi_parse.c
index 99d6dcb..29d0dbf 100644
--- a/src/libbluray/bdnav/clpi_parse.c
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdnav/clpi_parse.c
@@ -577,6 +577,11 @@ clpi_access_point(const CLPI_CL *cl, uint32_t pkt, int next, int angle_change, u
     // Assumes that there is only one pid of interest
     entry = &cpi->entry[0];
 
+    if (entry->num_ep_fine < 1) {
+        *time = 0;
+        return 0;
+    }
+
     for (ii = 0; ii < entry->num_ep_coarse; ii++) {
         ref = entry->coarse[ii].ref_ep_fine_id;
         spn = (entry->coarse[ii].spn_ep & ~0x1FFFF) + entry->fine[ref].spn_ep;
-- 
